      {
        "id": 113062,
        "name": "jagarciao",
        "username": "jagarciao",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jagarciao/{size}/2010_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-08-12T12:53:15.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Pinecone3\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/pinecone3/48/2494_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Pinecone3:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The reason I think that she's talking about absolving the world is that she says \"absolution you shall deliver\" to her sword. &nbsp;And swords, well, they are used to kill people. &nbsp;So if the sword is simultaneously killing and taking an active role in the \"absolution,\" it doesn't make sense that she would be absolving herself&nbsp;<em>of&nbsp;</em>violence by committing violence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I always saw that as meaning that she was absolving those she used the sword against of&nbsp;<em>their</em> sins. So she, Fanatic, was releasing them of their guilt through purifying punishment, her sword, so in the end they could reach peace in the afterlife. She saw herself as an agent of good… the Ultimate agent of good, ridding the world of evil and redeeming, through Absolution, those who committed evil deeds.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>I see the broken sword as a symbol of her moderation. The evil can be punished and perhaps even redeemed without the need for such a final solution. Her sword is no longer the only means to Absolution.&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 73,
        "post_type": 1,
